WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.630
A colleague printed an AI

2
00:00:02.630 --> 00:00:04.630
answer for a science lesson.

3
00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:07.200
It was clear and confident.

4
00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:10.300
It also said to clean the lab with

5
00:00:10.300 --> 00:00:12.960
bleach mixed with vinegar, and that

6
00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.680
mix gives off a toxic gas.

7
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:18.900
Today: when AI's mistakes become

8
00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:21.140
risks, and how to match your

9
00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.520
check to what's at stake.

10
00:00:23.780 --> 00:00:25.900
You've seen why: a chatbot

11
00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:27.320
predicts likely words.

12
00:00:28.100 --> 00:00:30.120
Sounding sure is not the

13
00:00:30.120 --> 00:00:31.340
same as being checked.

14
00:00:32.090 --> 00:00:34.770
It can invent a source, repeat an

15
00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:37.590
out-of-date rule, or be weaker in a

16
00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:39.270
language with less text online.

17
00:00:40.640 --> 00:00:42.740
At school, some mistakes are harmless.

18
00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:46.480
A clumsy story idea costs nothing.

19
00:00:47.210 --> 00:00:50.250
But a wrong fact in a test, wrong advice

20
00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:53.690
about a pupil's health, or a wrong rule in

21
00:00:53.690 --> 00:00:55.490
a letter home can hurt someone.

22
00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:58.700
So use a stakes ladder.

23
00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:01.130
The more harm a mistake could

24
00:01:01.130 --> 00:01:02.930
do, the stronger the check.

25
00:01:03.530 --> 00:01:05.410
Low stakes: read it yourself.

26
00:01:06.270 --> 00:01:08.730
Medium: check it against the textbook.

27
00:01:09.380 --> 00:01:12.960
High stakes, like health, safety, marks,

28
00:01:13.400 --> 00:01:17.340
money or the law, need an expert or an

29
00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:20.180
official source, and a person decides.

30
00:01:21.610 --> 00:01:22.010
Quick check!

31
00:01:22.630 --> 00:01:25.690
An AI answer tells my class which wild

32
00:01:25.690 --> 00:01:27.530
berries are safe to eat on our nature

33
00:01:27.530 --> 00:01:27.810
walk.

34
00:01:28.410 --> 00:01:29.670
Which check does it need?

35
00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:33.060
An expert or an official guide.

36
00:01:33.760 --> 00:01:35.620
A wrong answer could poison a

37
00:01:35.620 --> 00:01:37.460
child, so this is high stakes.

38
00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:40.500
Asking the chatbot again only

39
00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:41.960
repeats the same patterns.

40
00:01:42.770 --> 00:01:45.390
And if nobody can check it, don't use it.

41
00:01:46.550 --> 00:01:48.870
There's a second trap, inside us.

42
00:01:49.540 --> 00:01:51.920
Automation bias is trusting an

43
00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.060
answer because a machine gave it.

44
00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:56.560
People skip checks they would never

45
00:01:56.560 --> 00:01:59.080
skip for a stranger's advice, just

46
00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:00.440
because it came from a computer.

47
00:02:01.710 --> 00:02:02.950
I see it with my pupils.

48
00:02:03.770 --> 00:02:05.490
They copy an AI answer into

49
00:02:05.490 --> 00:02:07.550
homework without reading it, even

50
00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:09.070
when it says something odd.

51
00:02:09.730 --> 00:02:12.950
So I ask one question: how do you know?

52
00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:15.430
Some checks work well.

53
00:02:16.060 --> 00:02:18.500
Ask for sources, then open them,

54
00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:20.980
because invented sources are common.

55
00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:23.320
Check numbers and names

56
00:02:23.320 --> 00:02:24.380
against the textbook.

57
00:02:25.030 --> 00:02:27.690
And for anything high stakes, ask the

58
00:02:27.690 --> 00:02:30.370
person whose job it is: a nurse, the

59
00:02:30.370 --> 00:02:32.010
head teacher, the exam board.

60
00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:35.270
In the app, an AI answer

61
00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:36.330
is cut into claims.

62
00:02:37.170 --> 00:02:39.970
Mark each one correct, wrong, or

63
00:02:39.970 --> 00:02:42.250
needs a source, and spot the

64
00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:44.110
confident mistake hiding among them.

65
00:02:45.270 --> 00:02:45.810
Your turn.

66
00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:48.880
An AI drafted a letter to parents

67
00:02:48.880 --> 00:02:50.400
about the new exam rules.

68
00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:52.740
What do you do before it goes out?

69
00:02:54.030 --> 00:02:56.570
Check every rule against the official

70
00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:59.690
notice: a wrong rule misleads every

71
00:02:59.690 --> 00:03:00.090
family.

72
00:03:00.800 --> 00:03:03.520
Remember: sure isn't checked.

73
00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:05.910
Match the check to the stakes.

74
00:03:06.680 --> 00:03:09.380
Watch for automation bias, and if you

75
00:03:09.380 --> 00:03:11.000
can't check it, don't use it.

76
00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:14.420
Try it in class tomorrow: build a

77
00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:15.740
stakes ladder on the board.

78
00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:19.590
Read out five AI answers, from a

79
00:03:19.590 --> 00:03:21.670
poem to advice about medicine.

80
00:03:22.390 --> 00:03:24.610
Groups place each one on a rung,

81
00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:26.190
and say how they'd check it.
